    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Model candidates under thesis_model_candidate_v1, stated compactly: persistence candidate point 246.7, p10 215, p50 246.7, p90 280, interval_method snapshot empirical, calibration_n 6, train cutoff latest inspected mirror snapshot, rejected because the resolver is the FY2026 registered snapshot after fiscal-year close; budget-scaled candidate point 550, p10 485, p50 550, p90 615, interval_method widened snapshot-change residual, calibration_n 6, train cutoff latest inspected mirror snapshot, selected because FY2026 DoD funding and late fiscal-year award flow imply a much larger post-close snapshot than current YTD mirror persistence."
    },
    {
      "kind": "math",
      "text": "Quantitative bridge: latest inspected current snapshot = 246.7 billion dollars. The selected point assumes the October registered snapshot reaches 57.2% of the 961.6 billion dollar FY2026 DoD request, equal to 550.0 billion dollars, or 2.23 times the latest inspected current snapshot. I choose 57.2% rather than the raw current 25.7% share because award obligations should accumulate through the fiscal-year close and visible reporting catch-up, while staying well below total DoD budget authority because not all budget authority appears as prime award obligations in the registered API field."
    },
    {
      "kind": "math",
      "text": "Snapshot changes in billions were 18.8, 64.3, 5.0, 27.2, and 1.6; their sample dispersion is about 25.1. For a post-close registered snapshot with DoD delay and late-year procurement concentration, I use a conservative manual widening for model-error uncertainty: sigma = 50.8, about 2.0 times observed intra-snapshot change dispersion. Half-width = 1.28*sigma = 65.0. Point = 550 based on budget-scaled snapshot prior; 80% interval = 550 - 65 to 550 + 65 = 485 to 615."
    },
    {
      "kind": "math",
      "text": "Prior/update/interval: prior = budget-scaled current USAspending-derived snapshot, historical sample = six inspected DoD current-total snapshots from 129.7 to 246.7 billion dollars plus FY2026 DoD budget context of 961.6 billion dollars total and 384 billion dollars acquisition funding; adjustment components = upward late-fiscal-year award flow and FY2026 defense funding increase, partially offset by 90-day DoD reporting lag that can leave October below the eventual fully reported FY total; interval method = widened snapshot-change residual with sigma = 50.8 and half-width roughly 1.28*sigma = 65.0, implying bounds 485 and 615."
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Counter-consideration: downside risk would land below the interval if the October registered API snapshot still excludes most late-FY DoD contract actions because of the 90-day delay or if award obligations are shifted into account spending not captured by prime awards. Upside risk would land above the interval if reconciliation-funded acquisition and shipbuilding obligations hit USAspending before the snapshot or if a large September obligation wave is visible earlier than usual."
